course: religion-ethics
lesson: 75
title: Is it fair? Why bad things happen to good people
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Ruby's aunt was kind and helped everyone, and she died of cancer at 40, while Kai's grumpy grandad lived to 95; Ruby says «It's not fair», and she is right: how long someone lives is not a score for being good, but depends on health, chance and many things nobody controls.
explain: «If God is good, why are there wars and illness?» is one of the oldest hard questions, which philosophers call «the problem of evil»; many Christians answer that God gave people free will and people sometimes choose to hurt each other, and that God is with us in pain because Jesus suffered too.
explain: The Bible does not hide the question: in the book of Job a good man loses everything, his friends say it must be his fault, God says the friends are wrong, and Job meets God without getting a simple answer; Jesus also said a man born blind was not being punished for anyone's sin.
explain: People answer differently: Holly's grandma says «Everything happens for a reason», her dad is not so sure, and Jack's philosopher uncle says «Maybe there is no reason, and we must make our own meaning»; even the vicar says honestly that sometimes he doubts.
explain: Christians remember that Jesus himself cried on the cross, «My God, why have you forsaken me?», so doubt and anger can sit inside faith, and people of every belief can turn anger at unfairness into helping, as when Christians care for orphans.
explain: For a child today this means: you may say «it's not fair», ask hard questions without being afraid, and look for what you can do to help.
fact: How long someone lives is not a reward or a punishment; it depends on health, chance and many things nobody controls.
fact: Sometimes very good people die young, and nobody fully understands why.
fact: «Why does a good God allow suffering?» is called the problem of evil, and philosophers have discussed it for thousands of years.
fact: Many Christians answer that God gave people free will, and people sometimes choose to hurt each other, for example in wars.
fact: Christians do not believe illness or death is a punishment for being bad; Jesus said a man born blind was not being punished for anyone's sin.
fact: In the book of Job, a good man loses everything; his friends blame him, God says they are wrong, and Job gets no simple answer.
fact: Some babies are born with illnesses that doctors cannot cure; it is very sad and nobody's fault.
fact: Some people believe God has a plan and brings good out of bad; others believe some things just happen; people disagree.
fact: Some philosophers say life may have no given reason and we make our own meaning; humanists say meaning comes from how we live and love.
fact: Many people of faith have doubts; Christians remember that Jesus cried out on the cross, «My God, why have you forsaken me?»
fact: The Bible tells Christians to «look after orphans and widows», and many faiths run charities for children without parents.
fact: Feeling angry about unfairness is natural, and that anger can lead people to help others or change things.
exercise: Ruby's kind aunt dies at 40. Ruby says it's not fair. What is true? | It isn't fair, and nobody fully understands why | It was fair, because she must have done wrong | Kind people always die young as a reward | It is fair, because 40 is very old
exercise: Kai's grumpy grandad lived to 95; his kind neighbour died at 50. What does this show? | Lifespan isn't a score for goodness | Being grumpy makes you live longer | Kind people are punished with short lives | God rewards grumpy people with long lives
exercise: What do philosophers call the question «Why does a good God allow suffering?» | The problem of evil | The golden rule | The free will defence | The meaning of life
exercise: Ethan asks why God lets wars happen. What do many Christians answer? | God gave people free will, and people choose to hurt | God wants wars, to punish bad countries | Wars are started by angels, not people | God is too far away to know about them
exercise: Oscar asks if people die because God is punishing them. What do Christians teach? | No; death isn't a punishment, everyone dies | Yes; only bad people die before 80 | Yes; illness is always a punishment for sin | No, because God doesn't know who is bad
exercise: What did Jesus say about a man born blind? | He was not being punished for anyone's sin | His parents had done something bad, so he was born blind | He was blind because he was lazy and never washed his eyes | He would see again if he gave all his money to the temple
exercise: In the story of Job, what do Job's friends say? | That his suffering must be his own fault | That he should go on a long holiday | That God has forgotten about him | That he is the best man in the world
exercise: How does the book of Job end? | Job meets God but gets no simple answer | Job finds out it was all his own fault from the start | Job's friends are proved right, and Job says sorry to them | Job stops believing in God for ever and leaves his family
exercise: Leo's baby cousin died a few days after birth. Whose fault was it? | Nobody's; some illnesses can't be cured | The baby's, for being too small | The parents', for not praying enough | The doctors', who should cure everything
exercise: Holly's grandma says «Everything happens for a reason»; her dad isn't sure. What's true? | People disagree; it's okay to wonder | Grandma is right and science proves it | Dad is right and religion proves it | Nobody has ever thought about this
exercise: Mia asks if it's fair that some kids have no parents. What is true? | It's not fair, so caring for orphans matters | It is fair, because life is always fair in the end, for everybody | It is their own fault for being naughty | Only grown-ups need parents
exercise: What does the Bible tell Christians about orphans? | Look after orphans and widows | Orphans should look after themselves | Orphans cannot go to church | Only priests may help orphans
exercise: Lily asks the vicar if he ever doubts God when people die. What does he honestly say? | Sometimes he does; doubt is part of thinking deeply | Never; real vicars never have any doubts, not even when children die | Yes, so he has stopped believing in God | He refuses to talk about doubts
exercise: What did Jesus cry out on the cross? | «My God, why have you forsaken me?» | «I am the light of the world» | «Let the little children come to me» | «Love your neighbour as yourself»
exercise: Jack's uncle says, «Maybe there is no reason, and we must make our own meaning.» Who else says meaning comes from how we live? | Humanists | Hindus | Muslims | Catholic bishops
exercise: Freddie asks if it's okay to be angry about unfair deaths. What is true? | Yes; that anger can lead to helping others | No; being angry about anything is always a sin | No; every death is always fair, if you look closely | Yes, and he should stay angry about it for ever and ever
test: «God took Auntie coz He needed another angel,» says a lady at the funeral. Ruby frowns. Which reply fits what this lesson teaches? | People say that to comfort, but Christian teaching doesn't say people become angels | It's exactly right; God takes kind people early | God was punishing the family for something | It means Ruby should stop being sad
test: «If God's real and good, why's there wars? Checkmate, bro,» says a Gen Z cousin in Florida. What's a fair answer? | It's a hard question; many Christians point to free will | There's no answer to it, so the question is stupid and pointless | Wars are God's punishment for bad countries | Wars don't really happen any more
test: The vicar answers, very formally: «I shall not pretend to understand why; I believe, nonetheless, that God is present in our suffering.» What is he saying? | He doesn't know why, but believes God is with us | He knows exactly why it happened and will explain it later | God caused all the suffering on purpose, to test people | God is far away and absent whenever people are suffering
test: Grumpy Grandad lived to 95 and kind Mrs Lee died at 50. A boy in Newcastle says, «Being nice is pointless then, howay» (howay = come on). Which is the best reasoning? | Goodness isn't paid in years; it's worth it for its own sake | He's right; only grumpy people live long, so being kind is a waste of time | Kind people always live longer really | Mrs Lee must have secretly been bad
test: What would happen if everyone believed illness was a punishment for being bad? | Sick people would be blamed unfairly | Hospitals would get much better | People would stop getting ill, because they would all try harder to be good | Nothing; it would be harmless
test: Job's friends say his troubles must be his fault. In the story, who is right? | Not the friends; God says they were wrong | The friends, because suffering always means sin | Job himself, who admits that it was all his fault | Job's wife, who tells him to give up on God
test: «Me baby cousin only lived three days, it's not right,» says Leo from the Black Country, «I'm proper gutted» (gutted = heartbroken). Which reply is both honest and kind? | It isn't fair, and it's nobody's fault; she was loved | It was fair; God needed her more | It was her parents' fault for not praying | Babies don't count, so don't be sad
test: «Do u ever doubt God, vicar? Like for real tho?» asks Lily. If he says «sometimes», what does that show? | Doubt can be part of honest faith | He is a bad vicar and should quit | He doesn't believe in God at all | Vicars are not allowed to have doubts
test: A cartoon says, «Good guys always win and bad guys always get what they deserve.» What does real life show? | Life isn't always fair; good people can suffer | Real life works exactly like the cartoons, if you wait long enough | Bad people always die young | Good people never get ill
test: «Everything happens for a reason, sweetie,» says Holly's gran; her dad mutters, «Not so sure, Mum.» Who is right? | They disagree on a question people still argue about | Gran, because older people are always right | Dad, because science has proved there is no reason for anything | Neither; the question has an easy answer
test: «I'm well angry it's not fair, is that bad?» asks Freddie. Which answer turns anger to good? | It's natural; it can push you to help others | Yes, being angry about a death is always very bad | No, so shout at people until the anger passes | Hide it, because nobody should ever see you angry
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the relationship between moral conduct and length of life? | The duration of a life is neither a reward for virtue nor a penalty for vice | Persons of good character invariably enjoy longer lives | Persons of ill temper are invariably granted longer lives | Length of life is determined solely by attendance at worship
exam: Identify the term employed by philosophers to denote the question of why a good God permits suffering. | The problem of evil | The golden rule | The categorical imperative | The communion of saints
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the book of Job. | A righteous man suffers grievously, his friends' accusations are rejected, and no simple explanation is given | A wicked man is punished in proportion to his offences | Job's friends are vindicated in their assertion of his guilt | Job renounces his faith and is thereafter rewarded
exam: It is given that a believer entertains doubts following a bereavement. Ascertain which statement is consistent with the matters taught. | Doubt is common among persons of faith and may coexist with belief | Doubt demonstrates that the person never held any genuine faith whatsoever | Doubt is prohibited by every Christian denomination | Doubt may be entertained only by members of the clergy
